---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// The comprehensive FullBars reference — the Handlebars-faithful surface. Modelled
// on the handlebarsjs.com guide's spine (expressions → paths → block helpers →
// built-ins → subexpressions → custom helpers → partials → comments) but NOT a
// copy: it leads with what works exactly like Handlebars, then a named "Where
// FullBars diverges" section sourced from fullbars-compat.adoc. Prose is
// illustrative only; every runnable example comes from the single gate-validated
// source ../fullbars.mjs and renders through the real `surface` engine. The
// ~80-helper prelude is NOT re-listed here (that is the generated catalog's job) —
// this page teaches the language and the six built-ins, and links the catalog.
import Reference from "../layouts/Reference.astro";
import OpenInLab from "../components/OpenInLab.jsx";
import { examples as ex } from "../fullbars.mjs";

const SPEC_BASE = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_SPEC_BASE || "/flatbars/";

// Sticky left-nav: FullBars concepts, then the sibling surfaces, then the Lab.
const groups = [
  {
    label: "FullBars",
    items: [
      { href: "#intro", label: "What FullBars is" },
      { href: "#expressions", label: "Expressions & escaping" },
      { href: "#paths", label: "Paths" },
      { href: "#conditionals", label: "if / unless" },
      { href: "#each", label: "each" },
      { href: "#with", label: "with" },
      { href: "#lookup", label: "lookup & log" },
      { href: "#subexpressions", label: "Subexpressions" },
      { href: "#helpers", label: "Custom helpers" },
      { href: "#partials", label: "Partials" },
      { href: "#comments", label: "Comments" },
      { href: "#conventions", label: "Style & conventions" },
      { href: "#compile", label: "Compiles to JS" },
      { href: "#divergences", label: "Where it diverges" },
      { href: "#next", label: "Compatibility & next" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Other surfaces",
    items: [
      { href: "/minbars", label: "MinBars — Mustache" },
      { href: "/rawbars", label: "RawBars — the core" },
      { href: "/maxbars", label: "MaxBars — operators" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Play",
    items: [{ href: "/lab/index.html?engine=fullbars", label: "FlatBars Lab ↗" }],
  },
];

// Inline tag references — kept in a helper so Astro never parses the {{ }}.
const t = (s) => s;
---

<Reference
  title="FullBars — Handlebars-faithful"
  subtitle="FullBars is FlatBars's reference engine and its Handlebars-style surface: {{ }} auto-escaping, dotted paths, block helpers, @data, and `as |x|`. This runnable reference walks the language the way a Handlebars author already thinks — then names the handful of places FullBars deliberately diverges."
  groups={groups}
  spec="surface"
>
  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Intro ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intro">
    <h2>What FullBars is</h2>
    <p>
      <b>FullBars is the Handlebars-faithful member of the FlatBars family.</b> If
      you know Handlebars, you already know most of this page: <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code>
      auto-escapes, dotted paths read nested data, <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> /
      <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code> are block helpers, and partials, subexpressions, and
      <code>{t("{{#*inline}}")}</code> all work as you'd expect.
    </p>
    <p>
      What's underneath is different, and it's the whole point of the project.
      FullBars isn't a separate engine — it's a <b>surface syntax</b> over the same
      meaning-free core that runs <a href="/minbars">MinBars (Mustache)</a> and
      <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a>. One parser, four languages. In the core there
      are <b>no variables</b>: <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code> doesn't read a variable,
      it <em>applies</em> the helper <code>name</code> (see <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a>).
      That model holds the line in a few places where Handlebars is looser — gathered
      under <a href="#divergences">Where FullBars diverges</a>.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.hello.template} data={ex.hello.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      The panes are live: edit the <b>template</b> or the <b>data</b> and the output
      re-renders as you type. <b>Open in Lab</b> carries the example over to the full
      editor.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────── Expressions & escaping ───────────────────── -->
  <section id="expressions">
    <h2>Expressions &amp; escaping</h2>
    <p>
      The workhorse tag. <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code> applies <code>name</code> and
      inserts the result, <b>HTML-escaped by default</b> so data can't inject markup.
      To emit raw markup use the triple-stash <code>{t("{{{name}}}")}</code>. (Handlebars's
      <code>{t("{{&name}}")}</code> is accepted as an alias, but the triple-stash is the
      canonical FullBars spelling.)
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.escaping.template} data={ex.escaping.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────────── Paths ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="paths">
    <h2>Paths</h2>
    <p>
      Reach into nested objects with dots — <code>{t("{{user.address.city}}")}</code> —
      and <code>{t("{{this}}")}</code> is the current context.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.dotted.template} data={ex.dotted.data} />
    <h3>Climbing out: <code>../</code> and <code>@root</code></h3>
    <p>
      Inside a block, <code>{t("{{../x}}")}</code> climbs to the enclosing context and
      <code>{t("{{@root.x}}")}</code> jumps to the top-level data — no matter how deep
      the nesting.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.paths.template} data={ex.paths.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── if / unless ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="conditionals">
    <h2>Conditionals: <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, <code>unless</code></h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{#if x}} … {{else}} … {{/if}}")}</code> is a <b>block helper</b>: its
      head is a helper application, not a bare key. The <code>{t("{{else if …}}")}</code>
      chain works too. To test more than a bare value, nest a <a href="#subexpressions">subexpression</a>
      — <code>{t("{{#if (gte score 50)}}")}</code> is how FullBars writes "score ≥ 50"
      (the infix <code>{t(">=")}</code> belongs to <a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a>).
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.ifBlock.template} data={ex.ifBlock.data} />
    <h3><code>unless</code></h3>
    <p><code>{t("{{#unless x}}")}</code> renders when <code>x</code> is falsy — the explicit inverse of <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code>.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.unless.template} data={ex.unless.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────────── each ────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="each">
    <h2><code>each</code> — iteration</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{#each items}}")}</code> repeats its body once per element, pushing each
      element as the context. Inside, the <b><code>@data</code> variables</b>
      <code>{t("@index")}</code>, <code>{t("@first")}</code>, and <code>{t("@last")}</code>
      describe the position.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.eachList.template} data={ex.eachList.data} />
    <h3>Over an object</h3>
    <p>Iterating an object, <code>{t("@key")}</code> is the property name and <code>{t("{{this}}")}</code> the value.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.eachObject.template} data={ex.eachObject.data} />
    <h3>The empty case</h3>
    <p><code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> has its own <code>{t("{{else}}")}</code> for an empty list — no separate inverted section.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.eachElse.template} data={ex.eachElse.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      Rename the loop bindings with block params: <code>{t("{{#each xs as |item i|}}")}</code>
      gives the element and index your own names — useful inside nested loops.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────────── with ────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="with">
    <h2><code>with</code> — re-root the context</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{#with obj}}")}</code> makes <code>obj</code> the context for its body,
      so a deep path is written once.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.withBlock.template} data={ex.withBlock.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── lookup & log ───────────────────────── -->
  <section id="lookup">
    <h2><code>lookup</code> &amp; <code>log</code></h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{lookup obj key}}")}</code> reads a field whose name isn't known until
      render — an array index, or a key chosen by the data.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.lookup.template} data={ex.lookup.data} />
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{log x}}")}</code> writes to the console and emits nothing — a debugging
      aid that renders to the empty string.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Subexpressions ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="subexpressions">
    <h2>Subexpressions</h2>
    <p>
      Parentheses nest one helper's result into another's arguments:
      <code>{t("(lookup user \"name\")")}</code>. This is how FullBars avoids
      "write a JS helper for everything" — you <b>compose the ~80 helpers that ship</b>.
      Below, the looked-up name is upper-cased in one expression.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.subexpr.template} data={ex.subexpr.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Custom helpers ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="helpers">
    <h2>Custom helpers</h2>
    <p>
      When the prelude isn't enough, a host registers its own with the
      Handlebars-style <code>registerHelper(name, fn)</code> — the single most common
      Handlebars extension, and it runs <b>live right here</b>. Edit the
      <b>helpers.js</b> pane below: <code>loud</code> returns a string (escaped in
      <code>{t("{{ }}")}</code>), and <code>shout</code> returns <code>safe(…)</code> to
      emit raw markup (the <code>SafeString</code> equivalent).
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.customHelper.template} data={ex.customHelper.data} helpers={ex.customHelper.helpers} />
    <h3>Hash arguments</h3>
    <p>
      Trailing <code>key=value</code> pairs arrive as one options object — the natural
      shape for a helper with named options.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.customHelperHash.template} data={ex.customHelperHash.data} helpers={ex.customHelperHash.helpers} />
    <div class="callout info">
      <p>
        Two honest limits (<a href={`${SPEC_BASE}adr-0018-user-defined-helpers.html`}>ADR-018</a>):
        custom helpers are <b>inline only</b> — a custom <em>block</em> helper
        (<code>{t("{{#my}}…{{/my}}")}</code>) is out of scope, because it has no compiled
        equivalent yet. And declaring an arity
        (<code>registerHelper('loud', fn, 1)</code>) gives the same arity diagnostics as a
        built-in. In the <b>Lab</b>, helper code runs in a Worker (no page access), not a
        full sandbox — adequate for a playground.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Partials ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="partials">
    <h2>Partials</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{> name}}")}</code> includes another template and <b>inherits the caller's
      context</b> — the natural unit of reuse.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.partial.template} data={ex.partial.data} partials={ex.partial.partials} />
    <h3>A partial per row</h3>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.partialList.template} data={ex.partialList.data} partials={ex.partialList.partials} />
    <h3>Dynamic partials</h3>
    <p>The name can be an expression resolved at render time — here a language picked from the data.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.dynamicPartial.template} data={ex.dynamicPartial.data} partials={ex.dynamicPartial.partials} />
    <h3>Inline &amp; block partials</h3>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{#*inline \"name\"}}…{{/inline}}")}</code> defines a partial inline; a block
      partial <code>{t("{{#> layout}}…{{/layout}}")}</code> hands its body to the partial,
      which drops it in with <code>{t("{{> @partial-block}}")}</code>.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.inlinePartial.template} data={ex.inlinePartial.data} />
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.blockPartial.template} data={ex.blockPartial.data} partials={ex.blockPartial.partials} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Comments ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="comments">
    <h2>Comments</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{! … }}")}</code> is dropped from the output. The
      <code>{t("{{!-- … --}}")}</code> form may itself contain <code>{t("}}")}</code>.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.comment.template} data={ex.comment.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────── Style & conventions ─────────────────────── -->
  <section id="conventions">
    <h2>Style &amp; conventions</h2>
    <p>
      None of this changes what a template <em>means</em> — it changes how readable it
      is six months later. The examples on this page follow these conventions; so does
      the FlatBars Lab.
    </p>
    <ul class="conv">
      <li>
        <b>Tight delimiters.</b> Write <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code>, not
        <code>{t("{{ name }}")}</code> — no padding inside the stash. (The parser
        requires it for block tags anyway: <code>{t("{{ #each }}")}</code> is a lex
        error, so tight is the only form that's uniform across every tag.)
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Indent with the HTML.</b> A tag's indentation should match the markup around
        it — pick 2 or 4 spaces and keep it. Block bodies indent one level past their
        opening tag.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Block helpers get their own line.</b> Put <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code>,
        <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code>, and friends on a line by themselves rather than
        inlining them mid-markup. As a bonus, a block tag alone on its line is
        <em>standalone</em>: the engine trims that whole line, so the output stays tidy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Close tags align with their opener.</b> Every <code>{t("{{/if}}")}</code> /
        <code>{t("{{/each}}")}</code> sits on its own line, indented to match the
        <code>{t("{{#…}}")}</code> it closes — the body is what's indented between them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Comment with <code>{t("{{! … }}")}</code>, not <code>&lt;!-- … --&gt;</code>.</b>
        A Handlebars comment is dropped before render, so template notes never leak into
        the output; an HTML comment ships to the browser.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Reach for <code>~</code> sparingly.</b> A tilde (<code>{t("{{~name~}}")}</code>)
        trims the whitespace next to a tag — useful when readable indentation would
        otherwise show up in the output, but easy to overuse into line-noise. Format for
        humans first; add tildes only where the output genuinely needs them.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.whitespace.template} data={ex.whitespace.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────────── Compiles to JS ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="compile">
    <h2>Compiles to JS</h2>
    <p>
      Like Handlebars's precompilation, FullBars <b>compiles a template to a JS
      module</b> — and FlatBars gates that the compiled module and the interpreter
      produce <b>byte-identical</b> output (so the pane below is the same render, just
      ahead of time). Expand <b>Compiled JS</b> on the iteration example:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="fullbars" template={ex.eachList.template} data={ex.eachList.data} compile />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────── Where FullBars diverges ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="divergences">
    <h2>Where FullBars diverges from Handlebars</h2>
    <p>
      Most of Handlebars works unchanged. A few things differ <b>by design</b> — each a
      consequence of the meaning-free core, each with a one-line replacement. The
      normative list is <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}fullbars-compat.html`}>fullbars-compat.adoc</a>;
      this table is the short version.
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Handlebars</th><th>FullBars</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>else</code> as a keyword</td>
          <td><code>else</code> is a <b>separator</b>; <code>elif</code> is a real clause</td>
          <td>The core has no keywords (not even <code>else</code>); a block splits on a name-agnostic separator.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code> / <code>null</code> literals</td>
          <td>the same spellings — but they're <b>helpers</b></td>
          <td>The core's only literals are strings and numbers; <code>true</code>/<code>false</code>/<code>null</code> aren't keywords but nullary helpers.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mustache set delimiters <code>{t("{{=<% %>=}}")}</code></td>
          <td>fixed <code>{t("{{ }}")}</code> / <code>{t("{{{ }}}")}</code></td>
          <td>A fixed grammar keeps the parser meaning-free and the tooling simple. (A Mustache feature; Handlebars fixes the delimiters too.)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Data/lambdas as functions in the context</td>
          <td>a <a href="#helpers">helper</a>, or <code>apply</code></td>
          <td>A value is pure data, never a function; behaviour lives in the helper environment.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="tip">
      Coming from Mustache or Handlebars? An <b>implicit section</b> just works:
      <code>{t("{{#person}}…{{/person}}")}</code> renders once when <code>person</code> is
      truthy and <code>{t("{{#tags}}…{{/tags}}")}</code> iterates a list — FullBars looks an
      unregistered block head up as data and dispatches it like Handlebars'
      <code>blockHelperMissing</code> (array&nbsp;⇒&nbsp;<code>each</code>,
      truthy&nbsp;⇒&nbsp;<code>with</code>, empty&nbsp;⇒&nbsp;<code>{t("{{else}}")}</code>).
      Writing <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code> / <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> explicitly is still
      the clearer style. FullBars matches Handlebars on <b>96%</b> of a differential
      conformance corpus (rendered through the real Handlebars engine); the only gaps are
      functions-in-data and user-defined <em>block</em> helpers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────── Compatibility & next ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="next">
    <h2>Compatibility &amp; where to go next</h2>
    <p>
      The examples above are the Handlebars guide's own building blocks — expressions,
      paths, the built-in helpers, subexpressions, partials, custom helpers — running
      <b>unchanged on FullBars</b>. The complete concept-by-concept mapping (with the
      core each one desugars to) is the normative
      <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}appendix-handlebars.html`}>Handlebars → FlatBars appendix</a>.
    </p>
    <h3>The other ~70 helpers</h3>
    <p>
      This page tours the six built-ins; the rest of the prelude — string, array, math,
      and comparison helpers like <code>uppercase</code>, <code>join</code>,
      <code>add</code>, <code>sortBy</code>, <code>eq</code> — lives in the generated
      <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}prelude.html`}>helper catalog</a>, which is the single source
      of truth (it can't drift from the engine).
    </p>
    <h3>Up and down the ladder</h3>
    <p>
      <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a> is what FullBars desugars <em>to</em> — the
      meaning-free core. <a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a> adds infix operators and pipes
      on top — so <code>{t("{{#if (gte score 50)}}")}</code> here becomes
      <code>{t("{{#if score >= 50}}")}</code> there. Same engine, same compiler. Or jump
      straight into the <a href="/lab/index.html?engine=fullbars">Lab</a> and start typing.
    </p>
  </section>
</Reference>
